! Verbal 了 (le): perfective viewpoint

Verbal 了 commonly presents a bounded event as a whole. It is ''not'' a generic past-tense suffix.

* 我吃了饭。 — Wǒ chī le fàn. — I ate / have eaten.
* 我昨天吃了饭。 — Wǒ zuótiān chī le fàn. — I ate yesterday.

Mandarin also has a clause-final 了 often associated with a new situation or change of state. These are related in form but should not be collapsed into one English gloss.

Event boundedness, negation, complements, and sentence structure influence the distribution of 了. These examples are introductory rather than a complete licensing rule.

